  "statement": "Let $y=\\ln\\!\\left(\\dfrac{1+x}{1-x}\\right)$. Show that $\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=\\dfrac{2}{1-x^{2}}$.",
  "solution": "Using log laws, $y=\\ln(1+x)-\\ln(1-x)$. Then $\\dfrac{dy}{dx}=\\dfrac{1}{1+x}-\\dfrac{-1}{1-x}=\\dfrac{1}{1+x}+\\dfrac{1}{1-x} =\\dfrac{(1-x)+(1+x)}{1-x^{2}}=\\dfrac{2}{1-x^{2}}$.",
  "hints": [
    "Split the logarithm into a difference of two logs.",
    "Differentiate each term and combine over a common denominator."
  ],
